# Custom Snort Detection Rules
# Author: Inkwang Lee
# Purpose: Network security monitoring and threat detection
# Course: CAB441 - Network Security (QUT)
# Date: October 2025

# ================================================
# Rule 1: ICMP Traffic Detection
# Purpose: Monitor ICMP traffic for reconnaissance activities
# MITRE: Reconnaissance
# ================================================
alert icmp any any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"ICMP Traffic Detected"; sid:1000001; rev:1;)

# ================================================
# Rule 2: SSH Brute Force Attack Detection
# Purpose: Detect automated SSH password guessing attempts
# MITRE: T1110 - Brute Force
# ================================================
alert tcp any any -> $HOME_NET 22 (msg:"Possible SSH Brute Force Attack"; threshold:type limit, track by_src, count 5, seconds 60; sid:1000002; rev:1;)

# ================================================
# Rule 3: Port Scan Detection
# Purpose: Identify network reconnaissance via port scanning
# MITRE: T1046 - Network Service Discovery
# ================================================
alert tcp any any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"Possible Port Scan Detected"; flags:S; threshold:type threshold, track by_src, count 20, seconds 10; sid:1000003; rev:1;)

# ================================================
# Rule 4: ICMP Flood DDoS Detection
# Purpose: Detect ICMP-based denial of service attacks
# MITRE: T1498 - Network Denial of Service
# ================================================
alert icmp any any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"Possible ICMP Flood DDoS Attack"; threshold:type both, track by_src, count 50, seconds 5; sid:1000004; rev:1;)

# ================================================
# Configuration Notes:
# - $HOME_NET should be defined in snort.conf
# - Thresholds may need tuning based on your environment
# - Test rules in a controlled environment before production deployment
# ================================================
